<p>Marathon face-to-face talks between the United States and Iran in Islamabad ended without an agreement after about 21 hours, but both sides and Pakistan said diplomacy is still open. Vice President JD Vance said the U.S. had presented its “final and best offer” and blamed the impasse on Iran’s refusal to accept American terms, especially guarantees related to nuclear weapons. Iranian officials, meanwhile, said no one expected the first round to produce a full deal and argued the talks stalled because of what they called excessive or unreasonable U.S. demands. Even without a breakthrough, the meeting was significant. Multiple outlets described it as the highest-level direct U.S.-Iran engagement since 1979, with officials sitting at the same table in Pakistan instead of relying solely on shuttle mediation. Pakistan urged both sides to preserve the existing ceasefire and offered to keep facilitating dialogue, a modest but meaningful source of hope in a wider war that has already disrupted regional security and trade routes. The main sticking points were Iran’s nuclear program, sanctions relief, access to frozen funds, and control of or access through the Strait of Hormuz, where disruption has raised global concern.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/9EpA6gWRSOjExx1AFZELQ1XWwkTj8Eoocxl9wnGnBgcR9vjnmxozcyds05z9x5LHVK2h_Krg9z0Kxt27v_-xVMAqX8mAW2pwT90EJQU3nwye' alt='Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif and Foreign Minister Ishaq Dar greet Iran’s parliamentary speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf before the U.S.-Iran talks in Islamabad on April 11, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Pakistan role: Pakistani officials said Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif and army chief Asim Munir helped mediate several rounds of what Foreign Minister Ishaq Dar called intense, constructive negotiations. Vance later praised Pakistan’s leadership as “incredible hosts”.</li><li>Maritime dispute: One major sticking point was the Strait of Hormuz. Reporting said the dispute covered reopening shipping lanes, possible tolls, and competing claims over naval access and Iranian sea mines.</li><li>Time pressure: Al-Monitor reported that Washington had paused attacks for two weeks while negotiations played out, leaving only a narrow diplomatic window for follow-on talks after the first round ended without a deal.</li><li>Regional caution: As diplomacy unfolded, Pakistan also sent fighter jets to Saudi Arabia under a mutual defence pact, underscoring how fragile the ceasefire remains across the region.</li></ul><blockquote>It is imperative that the parties continue to uphold their commitment to the ceasefire. - Ishaq Dar</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: Vice President JD Vance said Washington left Islamabad with a “final and best offer” and insisted the U.S. needed a clear Iranian commitment not to seek a nuclear weapon or the tools to build one quickly. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/04/11/no-deal-vance-and-iranians-fail-to-reach-agreement-after-marathon-session-00868307'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Iran: Foreign Ministry spokesperson Esmaeil Baqaei said the talks ended without a deal because of U.S. “excessive demands,” while also saying Iran remained committed to diplomacy to protect national interests and the country’s well-being. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/talks-ended-without-deal-due-to-excessive-demands-made-by-us-iran/article70853441.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Pakistan: Foreign Minister Ishaq Dar said Pakistan was ready to keep facilitating engagement and dialogue, casting the Islamabad meeting as part of a broader push for durable peace and prosperity in the region and beyond. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/pakistan-to-continue-facilitating-us-iran-talks-says-ishaq-dar-urges-ceasefire/article70853340.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>Australia: Foreign Minister Penny Wong called the failed talks “disappointing” and said the priority must be to preserve the ceasefire and return quickly to negotiations. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/apr/12/penny-wong-calls-failed-peace-talks-between-us-and-iran-disappointing-and-urges-resumption'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>President Trump: President Trump said the United States would “win regardless” of whether the talks produced a deal, projecting public confidence even as negotiators left without an agreement. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/11/us-president-trump-says-us-wins-regardless-of-how-iran-talks-go'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/12/pakistan-urges-us-and-iran-to-uphold-ceasefire-after-talks-end'>Pakistan urges US and Iran to uphold ceasefire after talks end</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/control-of-the-strait-of-hormuz-and-irans-uranium-stockpiles-were-sticking-points'>Control of the Strait of Hormuz and Iran’s uranium stockpiles were sticking points</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/vance-says-talks-failed-reach-agreement-iran'>Vance says talks failed to reach agreement with Iran</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.economist.com/middle-east-and-africa/2026/04/11/america-and-iran-start-a-high-stakes-negotiation-to-end-their-war'>America and Iran start a high-stakes negotiation to end their war</a> - economist.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/55e4467b-19c3-433e-851c-2966f60c9f84'>Middle East war live: Vance says US and Iran fail to reach deal</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2026/apr/12/penny-wong-calls-failed-peace-talks-between-us-and-iran-disappointing-and-urges-resumption'>Penny Wong calls failed peace talks between US and Iran ‘disappointing’ and urges resumption</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/11/nx-s1-5779725/pakistan-hosts-historic-face-to-face-talks-between-th